---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import InstallCommand from "../../../components/InstallCommand.astro";
import { codeTheme } from "../../../components/code-theme";
import { fieldChildGuideExample, fieldStringsExample } from "../../../data/snippets";
import { exampleSource } from "../../../utils/example-source";
import signupForm from "../../../examples/SignupForm.tsx?raw";

const code = "mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Forms with Field | Buril UI"
  description="Label, describe and validate form controls with Field: the render prop, errors, required fields and your own language."
>
  <h1 class="font-display text-3xl">Forms with Field</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted-foreground">
    Field gives one control a visible label, help text and an error message, and wires them so
    screen readers read all three. You keep your own state and validation.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Quick path</h2>
  <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>Install Field and the controls you need.</li>
    <li>Wrap each control in a Field and spread the props it gives you onto the control.</li>
    <li>
      Pass the error message to <code>error</code> after you validate.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <InstallCommand
    id="install-forms"
    commands={["add @buril/button @buril/field @buril/input @buril/textarea"]}
  />

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">A complete form</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    A sign-up form that validates on submit, shows each error under its field and moves focus to the
    first field to fix. This code is type-checked and tested with the site.
  </p>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={exampleSource(signupForm)} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">The render prop</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    Field does not know your control. It hands you the props to put on it, as a render prop:
    <code data-wrap>{"{(control) => <Input {...control} />}"}</code>. They are:
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      <code>id</code>, which the label's <code>for</code> points at.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>aria-describedby</code>, listing the description and then the error, so they are read in
      that order after the name.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>aria-invalid</code>, while there is an error.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>required</code>, when the field is required.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-4">
    Spread all of them, after your own props. With a single element you can skip the function: Field
    adds the same props, keeps the element's own <code>id</code> and merges its
    <code>aria-describedby</code>.
  </p>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={fieldChildGuideExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Errors</h2>
  <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      Pass the message to <code>error</code>. An empty string, <code>null</code> or
      <code>undefined</code> means no error.
    </li>
    <li>
      The error shows with an icon and text, so color is never the only signal, and screen readers
      hear "Error:" before it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Its region is always on the page and uses <code>aria-live="polite"</code>, so a new error is
      announced when it appears. You do not need your own live region for field errors.
    </li>
    <li>
      Add <code>noValidate</code> to the <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> when you validate yourself, so
      the browser's own bubbles do not appear on top of Field's errors.
    </li>
    <li>
      On submit, move focus to the first field with an error. Its name, description and error are
      read together.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Required fields</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    <code>required</code> sets the native <code>required</code> on the control and shows
    "(required)" next to the label. The marker is hidden from screen readers, which already announce
    the native state, so nobody hears it twice.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4">
    If your render prop drops <code>required</code>, Field notices after rendering: the marker
    becomes part of the label's name again, so the requirement is still announced, and development
    builds print a warning. A custom control can report it with <code>aria-required</code> instead.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Your own language</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">Field writes two strings of its own. Pass them in your site's language:</p>
  <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      <code>requiredLabel</code>, the visible marker, "(required)" by default.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>errorPrefix</code>, the hidden word before the error, "Error:" by default.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={fieldStringsExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-4">
    The Link's <code>newTabLabel</code> works the same way for links that open a new tab.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Other controls</h2>
  <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      An{" "}
      <a href="/docs/components/input" class="underline underline-offset-4">
        Input
      </a>{" "}
      or a
      <a href="/docs/components/textarea" class="underline underline-offset-4">
        Textarea
      </a>{" "}
      goes inside a Field, and a
      <a href="/docs/components/select" class="underline underline-offset-4">
        Select
      </a>{" "}
      does too.
    </li>
    <li>
      A{" "}
      <a href="/docs/components/checkbox" class="underline underline-offset-4">
        Checkbox
      </a>{" "}
      or a
      <a href="/docs/components/switch" class="underline underline-offset-4">
        Switch
      </a>{" "}
      on its own goes inside a <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> with its text; use a Field when it needs
      help text or an error.
    </li>
    <li>
      A{" "}
      <a href="/docs/components/radio-group" class="underline underline-offset-4">
        Radio Group
      </a>{" "}
      is its own field: its <code>legend</code> is the label.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
